                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear Colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 89-year-old female patient, admitted on 19/03/1982 with acute lower abdominal pain and confirmed tubal pregnancy. On admission, clinical examination and transvaginal ultrasonography revealed a right-sided ectopic tubal pregnancy with signs of early rupture. Laboratory investigations including serum beta-hCG confirmed the diagnosis. Given her hemodynamic stability and the surgical risk, the patient was promptly taken to the operating theatre, where a laparoscopic salpingectomy was performed without complications. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. Vital signs remained stable throughout her stay. She was mobilized early with physiotherapy support and discharged home on 21/03/1982 in good general condition. Follow-up with yourself is recommended within one week. Please do not hesitate to contact me should you require further information.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
